menu "ESP32-C5 CSI Project Configuration"

    config CSI_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID"
        default "MyRouter"
        help
            Name of your 2.4 GHz WiFi network.

    config CSI_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi Password"
        default "mypassword"
        help
            WPA2 password of your WiFi network. Leave empty for an open network.

    config CSI_WIFI_MAX_RETRY
        int "Maximum connection retries"
        default 10

    config CSI_PING_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Ping interval in milliseconds"
        default 20
        range 5 1000
        help
            20 ms = 50 CSI packets/second.

    config CSI_LLTF_ONLY
        bool "Capture L-LTF only (one fixed CSI shape)"
        default n
        help
            Decision rule - use the output of Step 04:

              * If Step 04's histogram showed ONE shape with more than ~90%
                of the packets, leave this OFF. The auto-lock picks that
                dominant shape and you get the richest CSI available.

              * If Step 04's histogram FRAGMENTED across three or more shapes
                (mixed 11g / HT / HE traffic from your router), turn this ON.
                Only the legacy long training field is captured, which is
                present in every single packet regardless of PHY, so every
                packet then yields exactly the same number of subcarriers.
                You lose some resolution but gain a rock-steady stream.

    config CSI_CSV_DECIMATE
        int "Print 1 CSV line out of every N packets"
        default 5
        range 1 100
        help
            At 50 packets/s a full CSV dump of 64 subcarriers can swamp the
            serial console. 5 means ~10 lines/s, which plots nicely and still
            leaves the console readable.

    config CSI_LOCK_PACKETS
        int "Packets to observe before locking the CSI shape"
        default 200
        range 20 2000
        help
            The firmware watches this many packets, finds the most common
            CSI length, then accepts only that length from then on. Mixing
            lengths in one statistic is the classic way to get a detector
            that never triggers.

endmenu
